            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Introduction</strong><br>\nThe Yom Kippur War began on October 6, 1973, when a coalition of Arab states, led by Egypt and Syria, launched a surprise attack on Israel. This day was a high Jewish holiday known as Yom Kippur. The Arab forces took advantage of this day, as many Israelis were fasting and praying.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Preceding Tensions</strong><br>\nThe roots of the war can be traced back to long-standing conflicts and tensions between Israel and its Arab neighbors. The Six-Day War of 1967 was a particular trigger, where Israel captured territories previously controlled by Egypt, Syria, and Jordan. These territorial gains led to sustained tensions in the region.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Military Preparations</strong><br>\nBefore the war, the Arab states were militarily gearing up to regain the territories lost in the Six-Day War. With the support of the Soviet Union, they received modern weapons and military training. On the other hand, Israel was prepared for possible threats but was caught off guard by the timing of the attack.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          }
        ],
